+# Forking Base locally
+
+Short version: `yarn fork base` does **not** fork Base. It forks Ethereum
+mainnet, where our USDC address has no code — hence the empty reads. And
+`cast chain-id` cannot tell you otherwise, because the fork is pinned to
+31337 no matter what it forks.
+
+## What the onboarding note gets wrong
+
+### 1. `yarn fork base` ignores the `base` argument
+
+The chain of scripts, verbatim:
+
+`package.json` (repo root):
+
+```json
+"fork": "yarn foundry:fork",
+"foundry:fork": "yarn workspace @se-2/foundry fork",
+```
+
+`packages/foundry/package.json` (as originally written):
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}",
+```
+
+`${1:-mainnet}` looks like "use the first CLI argument, default to
+mainnet", but `$1` is never set: Yarn runs the script through its own
+shell with no positional parameters, and extra CLI words are not
+forwarded down the root → workspace → script chain anyway. `${1:-mainnet}`
+therefore always expands to `mainnet`, and the `base` you typed is
+dropped on the floor. Observed:
+
+```
+$ yarn fork base
+anvil --fork-url mainnet --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+Same result for `yarn fork FORK_URL=base`, `yarn workspace @se-2/foundry
+fork FORK_URL=base`, and (before the fix below) `FORK_URL=base yarn fork`
+— every one of them printed `--fork-url mainnet`. There is no argument
+you can append to `yarn fork` that changes the fork target.
+
+`mainnet` here is the alias from `packages/foundry/foundry.toml`:
+
+```toml
+mainnet = "https://eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io/v2/${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}"
+base    = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+So the node comes up as a healthy fork — of Ethereum mainnet.
+
+### 2. Why the USDC reads come back empty
+
+`0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` is USDC **on Base**. On
+Ethereum mainnet that address is an empty account. Every call to it
+returns `0x`, which viem/wagmi surfaces as an empty/failed read — exactly
+"as though the token were not deployed", because on the chain you
+actually forked, it is not. Nothing is wrong with the contract.
+
+### 3. Why `cast chain-id` proves nothing
+
+`packages/foundry/Makefile`:
+
+```make
+# Start a fork
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+The fork is **hard-pinned to 31337** so the SE-2 frontend
+(`targetNetworks: [chains.foundry]` in `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts`)
+and the `31337` deploy paths keep working. A correct Base fork reports
+31337; a mainnet fork reports 31337; a plain non-forking `yarn chain`
+anvil reports 31337. `cast chain-id` returning a number only proves an
+RPC is listening. The teammate who ran it got a true answer to a question
+that does not distinguish the failure.
+
+## The command that actually forks Base
+
+The `fork` script in `packages/foundry/package.json` has been changed to
+read the target from the environment instead of a phantom `$1`:
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${FORK_URL:-mainnet}",
+```
+
+With that, from the repo root:
+
+```bash
+FORK_URL=base yarn fork      # → anvil --fork-url base --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+`yarn fork` with no variable still defaults to mainnet, unchanged.
+`FORK_URL` takes any alias from the `[rpc_endpoints]` table in
+`foundry.toml` (`base`, `baseSepolia`, `optimism`, …) or a full RPC URL.
+
+If you are on a checkout without that fix, invoke make directly — use
+`yarn exec` so the Yarn-managed `shx` on which `setup-anvil-wallet`
+depends is on `PATH`:
+
+```bash
+cd packages/foundry && yarn exec make fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+Plain `make fork FORK_URL=base` fails with `setup-anvil-wallet Error 127`
+outside the Yarn environment.
+
+Note `https://mainnet.base.org` is a public rate-limited endpoint; for
+sustained forking, point `FORK_URL` at your own Base RPC URL.
+
+## The check that would have caught this
+
+Ask the chain for state that only Base has. Our own USDC address is the
+best probe, because it is the exact thing the contract depends on:
+
+```bash
+cast code 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+- long bytecode blob → you are on a fork of Base
+- `0x` → you are **not**, whatever `cast chain-id` says
+
+Confirm it is really the token, not just some deployed account:
+
+```bash
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "symbol()(string)" --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+# expect: USDC
+```
+
+This distinguishes Base from every other chain we might have forked:
+mainnet, Sepolia and a bare anvil have nothing at that address, and it is
+not one of the `0x42...` OP-Stack predeploys that Optimism, Base and
+every other OP chain share — so it will not pass on an Optimism fork
+either.
+
+Run against the node currently on port 8545 in this checkout:
+
+```
+$ cast chain-id  --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+31337                       # "looks fine"
+$ cast code 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+0x                          # not a Base fork
+```
+
+That is the whole bug, in two commands.
+
+A useful secondary signal: a real fork starts at the upstream chain's
+current height, so `cast block-number` should return a number in the
+tens of millions for Base. A handful of blocks means no fork at all.
